## 7. Middleware / route protection

There is **no file literally named `middleware.ts`**. Route protection is implemented in **`src/proxy.ts`**. I verified this isn't a stray/misnamed file: Next.js 16.3.4 has renamed the middleware convention to `proxy.ts` (grep of `node_modules/next/dist/build/index.js` shows Next.js now emits a deprecation warning for `middleware.ts` and errors if both files exist, directing you to use `proxy.ts` instead — see `nextjs.org/docs/messages/middleware-to-proxy`). So this file is the real, active middleware equivalent for this Next 16 app.

`src/proxy.ts`:
```ts
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";
import { auth } from "@/auth";

export default auth((req) => {
  const isLoggedIn = !!req.auth;

  if (!isLoggedIn) {
    const signInUrl = new URL("/signin", req.nextUrl.origin);
    signInUrl.searchParams.set("callbackUrl", req.nextUrl.pathname);
    return NextResponse.redirect(signInUrl);
  }
});

export const config = {
  matcher: ["/dashboard/:path*"],
};
```
It only protects `/dashboard/:path*`; nothing else (e.g. no API routes) is gated at the proxy/middleware layer.

